            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 65-year-old female, admitted on 12/05/1961 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. Laboratory investigations confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. A CT scan of the abdomen was subsequently performed, confirming the diagnosis without evidence of perforation. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy under general anaesthesia on 13/05/1961, which proceeded without complications. Postoperatively, she received intravenous fluid replacement, analgesic medication, and prophylactic antibiotics. Wound care was provided, and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient mobilised well and was discharged home on 16/05/1961 in good general condition. I recommend follow-up in two weeks for wound inspection and review of histopathological findings. Please do not hesitate to contact me if needed.</div>"
            },
